Fingerprint pinning suits the ESP8266 because full chain validation is heavy for its
heap. On an ESP32 prefer `setCACert()` — it survives certificate rotation.

```cpp
#include <Nodrix.h>
#include <esp_sleep.h>

#define SLEEP_MINUTES 15

NODRIX_WRITE("pump") {
  digitalWrite(PUMP_PIN, value.asBool());
}

void setup() {
  pinMode(PUMP_PIN, OUTPUT);
  Nodrix.beginHTTP(WIFI_SSID, WIFI_PASS, HOST, TOKEN);
  Nodrix.send("soil", analogRead(34));
  Nodrix.flush();
  Nodrix.poll();
  esp_sleep_enable_timer_wakeup((uint64_t)SLEEP_MINUTES * 60ULL * 1000000ULL);
  esp_deep_sleep_start();
}

void loop() {}
```

HTTP mode pairs `beginHTTP()` with `poll()`. Never call `run()` here — `run()` services
the WebSocket that HTTP mode does not open.

Yes. Handlers work identically in HTTP mode — the difference is when they fire. On an
always-on WebSocket the cloud pushes a write and your handler runs immediately. On a
sleepy device the write is queued and your handlers run during `Nodrix.poll()` on the
next wake.

```cpp
NODRIX_WRITE("interval_min") {
  if (!value.isNull()) sleepMinutes = value.asInt();
}
```

So a command sent while the board naps is applied when it next wakes, not lost.

At file scope, next to your includes — not inside `setup()` or `loop()`. The macro
declares the handler and registers it before `main()` runs, so there is nothing to wire
up yourself.

```cpp
#include <Nodrix.h>

NODRIX_WRITE("relay") {
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, value.asBool());
}

void setup() { Nodrix.begin(WIFI_SSID, WIFI_PASS, HOST, TOKEN); }
void loop()  { Nodrix.run(); }
```

You never call the handler and you never register it in `setup()`.

Register the callbacks before `begin()`, and read `Nodrix.connected()` any time you
need the current state.

```cpp
void setup() {
  Nodrix.onConnect([]()    { Serial.println("online");  });
  Nodrix.onDisconnect([]() { Serial.println("offline"); });
  Nodrix.begin(WIFI_SSID, WIFI_PASS, HOST, TOKEN);
}
```

