Register every network with `addAP()` first, then call the two-argument `begin()` that
takes only host and token. The strongest reachable network wins.

```cpp
#include <Nodrix.h>

const int REED_PIN = 14;
bool wasOpen = false;

void setup() {
  pinMode(REED_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP);
  Nodrix.begin(WIFI_SSID, WIFI_PASS, HOST, TOKEN);
}

void loop() {
  Nodrix.run();

  bool open = digitalRead(REED_PIN) == LOW;
  if (open && !wasOpen) Nodrix.event("door_opened");
  wasOpen = open;
}
```

Use `event()` for things that happen at a moment; use `send()` for values that have a
current reading.

Call the accessor that matches the type you want on the `value` handed to your handler:

```cpp
NODRIX_WRITE("setpoint") {
  if (value.isNull()) return;
  float target = value.asFloat();
  applySetpoint(target);
}
```

`NodrixValue` offers `asBool()`, `asInt()`, `asLong()`, `asFloat()`, `asDouble()` and
`asString()`. Guard with `isNull()` first — a device connecting before the variable has
ever been set would otherwise apply 0.

```cpp
#include <Nodrix.h>

const int LIVING  = 16;
const int BEDROOM = 17;
const int PORCH   = 18;

NODRIX_WRITE("light_living")  { digitalWrite(LIVING,  value.asBool()); }
NODRIX_WRITE("light_bedroom") { digitalWrite(BEDROOM, value.asBool()); }
NODRIX_WRITE("light_porch")   { digitalWrite(PORCH,   value.asBool()); }

void setup() {
  pinMode(LIVING,  OUTPUT);
  pinMode(BEDROOM, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(PORCH,   OUTPUT);
  Nodrix.begin(WIFI_SSID, WIFI_PASS, HOST, TOKEN);
}

void loop() {
  Nodrix.run();
}
```

One handler per bound variable. Relay boards are often active-LOW — invert the write if
yours is.

Not by hand — the library acks each write as it delivers it to your handler. Acking is
how the cloud learns the write landed so it can stop re-sending.

Because delivery is at-least-once, your handler can run twice for the same command. Keep
it idempotent: set state from `value` rather than toggling.

```cpp
NODRIX_WRITE("relay") {
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, value.asBool());
}
```

That is safe to run repeatedly. A handler that flipped the pin would not be.

